(   [Problem Statement]: Explain CPython's Memory Management model: Object Reference Counting, Generational Garbage Collection, and Object Interning.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   CPython automates memory lifecycle management via two coordinated mechanisms:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Reference Counting \(Primary Mechanism\):) Tj
T*
(   - Every `PyObject` contains an internal reference counter \(`ob_refcnt`\).) Tj
T*
(   - When a variable is assigned \(`a = [1, 2]`\) or passed to a function, `ob_refcnt` increments.) Tj
T*
(   - When a reference is reassigned, deleted \(`del a`\), or falls out of scope, `ob_refcnt`) Tj
T*
(   decrements.) Tj
T*
(   - When `ob_refcnt == 0`, the memory allocated to the object is immediately reclaimed and) Tj
T*
(   returned to CPython's memory allocator \(pymalloc\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Generational Garbage Collector \(Cyclic GC\):) Tj
T*
(   - Reference counting alone cannot detect Circular References \(e.g., Object A references B, and) Tj
T*
(   B references A; even if disconnected from global scope, their refcounts remain 1\).) Tj
T*
(   - CPython's cyclic GC periodically scans three generational object pools \(Gen 0, Gen 1, Gen 2\)) Tj
T*
(   to detect isolated circular dependency graphs and sweep unreferenced cycles.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Small Integer Interning:) Tj
T*
(   - CPython pre-allocates global singleton integer objects in the range `-5` to `256`. Repeated) Tj
T*
(   assignments point to the identical memory address \(`id\(a\) == id\(b\)`\), saving allocation time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
