/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Describe IEEE 754 Floating-Point representation in Python, floating-point rounding errors, and the math.isclose\(\) solution.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Python floats are implemented as 64-bit double-precision IEEE 754 numbers:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Root Cause of Rounding Inaccuracies:) Tj
T*
(   Computers store numbers in base-2 binary. Numbers like 0.1 and 0.2 have finite decimal) Tj
T*
(   representations but form repeating infinite fractions in binary \(similar to 1/3 in decimal =) Tj
T*
(   0.3333...\):) Tj
T*
(   - 0.1 in binary: 0.0001100110011...) Tj
T*
(   - Truncating to 53 bits of precision introduces minute rounding errors:) Tj
T*
(   `0.1 + 0.2` evaluates in Python to `0.30000000000000004`.) Tj
T*
(   - Consequently, `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` evaluates strictly to `False`!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Engineering Solutions:) Tj
T*
(   1. Never compare floats for absolute equality using `==` in engineering scripts.) Tj
T*
(   2. Use `math.isclose\(a, b, rel_tol=1e-9\)` to evaluate approximate equality within tolerance) Tj
T*
(   bounds.) Tj
T*
(   3. When financial or exact decimal precision is mandatory, use the `decimal` module:) Tj
T*
(   `Decimal\('0.1'\) + Decimal\('0.2'\) == Decimal\('0.3'\)`.) Tj
T*
ET

