(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Python Virtual Environment architecture \(venv\), detailing site-packages isolation and requirements.txt dependency management.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Managing Python dependencies across multiple embedded and engineering projects requires virtual) Tj
T*
(   isolation:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Dependency Conflict Problem:) Tj
T*
(   Project A \(IoT Sensor Gateway\) requires NumPy 1.21. Project B \(Machine Learning edge model\)) Tj
T*
(   requires NumPy 2.0. Installing globally in the operating system causes conflicting version) Tj
T*
(   crashes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Virtual Environment Architecture \(`python -m venv myenv`\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Isolation: Creates an isolated directory containing a dedicated Python executable binary,) Tj
T*
(   standard library symlinks, and a private `site-packages/` directory.) Tj
T*
(   2. Activation: Running `source myenv/bin/activate` \(Linux/Mac\) or `myenv\\Scripts\\activate`) Tj
T*
(   \(Windows\) prepends the virtual environment path to the system `PATH` variable.) Tj
T*
(   3. Dependency Pinning \(`requirements.txt`\):) Tj
T*
(   - Exporting installed packages: `pip freeze > requirements.txt`.) Tj
T*
(   - Replicating identical environments on laboratory test benches: `pip install -r) Tj
T*
(   requirements.txt`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
