12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze Python's Garbage Collection mechanism in detail, explaining how circular references are collected via Generational GC.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   While reference counting destroys objects immediately upon reaching refcount 0, it cannot) Tj
T*
(   handle Reference Cycles:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Circular Reference Trap:) Tj
T*
(   ```python) Tj
T*
(   class Node:) Tj
T*
(   def __init__\(self\):) Tj
T*
(   self.link = None) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   nodeA = Node\(\)) Tj
T*
(   nodeB = Node\(\)) Tj
T*
(   nodeA.link = nodeB # nodeA references nodeB) Tj
T*
(   nodeB.link = nodeA # nodeB references nodeA) Tj
T*
(   del nodeA) Tj
T*
(   del nodeB) Tj
T*
(   ```) Tj
T*
(   Even after deleting external references `nodeA` and `nodeB`, their internal reference counts) Tj
T*
(   remain 1. Without specialized handling, they would remain orphaned in RAM forever \(memory) Tj
T*
(   leak\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   CPython's Generational GC Solution:) Tj
T*
(   1. Generational Pools: Divides heap objects into Generation 0 \(youngest\), Generation 1, and) Tj
T*
(   Generation 2 \(oldest\).) Tj
T*
ET

