(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Integer Interning and String Interning in CPython, analyzing their performance benefits and identity test \(is vs ==\) implications.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Interning is an optimization technique where immutable objects are cached and reused as) Tj
T*
(   singletons:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Small Integer Interning:) Tj
T*
(   CPython pre-allocates an array of 262 integer objects covering integers from `-5` to `256`) Tj
T*
(   inclusive.) Tj
T*
(   Whenever an integer in this range is assigned, CPython returns a reference to the existing) Tj
T*
(   pre-allocated object:) Tj
T*
(   ```python) Tj
T*
(   x = 100) Tj
T*
(   y = 100) Tj
T*
(   print\(x is y\) # True \(points to the exact same pre-allocated memory address!\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   p = 1000) Tj
T*
(   q = 1000) Tj
T*
(   print\(p is q\) # False in interactive shell \(different heap allocations\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(p == q\) # True \(values are numerically identical\)) Tj
T*
(   ```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. String Interning:) Tj
T*
(   CPython automatically interns strings that look like Python identifiers \(alphanumeric with) Tj
T*
(   underscores\). Comparing interned strings via `is` takes constant $O\(1\)$ pointer comparison time) Tj
T*
(   rather than $O\(N\)$ character-by-character checking, dramatically accelerating dictionary) Tj
T*
(   lookups and attribute lookups.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 18:) Tj
T*
ET
