T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the difference between `is` and `==` operators with mutable and immutable data structures, writing code tests.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Distinguishing identity from equality in Python:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   ```python) Tj
T*
(   # 1. Immutable primitives \(interning\)) Tj
T*
(   a = 250) Tj
T*
(   b = 250) Tj
T*
(   print\('a == b:', a == b\) # True \(values match\)) Tj
T*
(   print\('a is b:', a is b\) # True \(points to identical pre-allocated object\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # 2. Mutable lists) Tj
T*
(   list1 = [1, 2, 3]) Tj
T*
(   list2 = [1, 2, 3]) Tj
T*
(   print\('list1 == list2:', list1 == list2\) # True \(values match\)) Tj
T*
(   print\('list1 is list2:', list1 is list2\) # False \(two distinct heap allocations!\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # 3. Aliasing \(Shared reference\)) Tj
T*
(   list3 = list1) Tj
T*
(   print\('list1 is list3:', list1 is list3\) # True \(identical memory reference\)) Tj
T*
(   list3.append\(4\)) Tj
T*
(   print\('list1 after mutation:', list1\) # [1, 2, 3, 4] \(mutated via alias!\)) Tj
T*
(   ```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
